{"networkAttributes":[{"KEGG_PATHWAY_IMAGE":"https://www.kegg.jp/kegg/pathway/spo/spo01210.png","KEGG_PATHWAY_ORG":"spo","modules_id":["Citrate cycle, first carbon oxidation, oxaloacetate => 2-oxoglutarate [PATH:spo01210]","Citrate cycle, second carbon oxidation, 2-oxoglutarate => oxaloacetate [PATH:spo01210]","Valine/isoleucine biosynthesis, pyruvate => valine / 2-oxobutanoate => isoleucine [PATH:spo01210]","Ornithine biosynthesis, glutamate => ornithine [PATH:spo01210]","Pyruvate oxidation, pyruvate => acetyl-CoA [PATH:spo01210]","Leucine biosynthesis, 2-oxoisovalerate => 2-oxoisocaproate [PATH:spo01210]"],"KEGG_PATHWAY_ID":"path:spo01210","classes":[""],"description":"2-Oxocarboxylic acids, also called 2-oxo acids and alpha-keto acids, are the most elementary set of metabolites that includes pyruvate (2-oxopropanoate), 2-oxobutanoate, oxaloacetate (2-oxosuccinate) and 2-oxoglutarate. This diagram illustrates the architecture of chain extension and modification reaction modules for 2-oxocarboxylic acids. The chain extension module RM001 is a tricarboxylic pathway where acetyl-CoA derived carbon is used to extend the chain length by one. The chain modification modules RM002 (including RM032) and RM033, together with a reductive amination step (RC00006 or RC00036), generate basic and branched-chain amino acids, respectively. The modification module RM030 is used in the biosynthesis of glucosinolates, a class of plant secondary metabolites, for conversion to oxime followed by addition of thio-glucose moiety. Furthermore, the chain extension from 2-oxoadipate to 2-oxosuberate is followed by coenzyme B biosynthesis in methonogenic archaea.","diseases":[""],"diseases_id":[""],"KEGG_PATHWAY_LINK":"https://www.kegg.jp/kegg-bin/show_pathway?spo01210","modules":["spo_M00010","spo_M00011","spo_M00019","spo_M00028","spo_M00307","spo_M00432"],"name":"2-Oxocarboxylic acid metabolism [spo01210]_1"}]},
